From 338db9dc6168e7afa146a9d8ba1bfd7d424efca3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Connor Tarabocchia <318465412+ctarabocchia@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 7 Sep 2026 20:28:15 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] PlatformAudio: configure software audio processing on
 desktop

`configure_audio_processing` only ever touched the hardware effects
(`enable_builtin_aec` and friends), which desktop has none of, so on
macOS, Windows and Linux the three switches were accepted and ignored:
WebRTC's software APM kept whatever the voice engine last applied, and
nothing in the public API could change it.

Two things set the APM's config in this build, both measured from
libwebrtc's own log, and the fix has to cover both:

- WebRtcVoiceEngine::Init applies its own defaults (AEC, NS, AGC all on)
  once, lazily, when the first PeerConnection is created. The bridge's
  PeerConnectionFactory therefore builds the APM itself
  (`BuiltinAudioProcessingBuilder().Build(env)` handed back through
  `CustomAudioProcessing`), keeps the handle, and `set_audio_processing`
  edits the three `enabled` flags on its live config -- so the high-pass
  filter and the analog gain controller's tuning survive -- remembering
  the switches and re-applying them after every PeerConnection is
  initialised.
- AudioRtpSender::SetSend hands the audio *source's* AudioOptions to the
  engine every time a track is sent (publish, unmute), and
  `create_device_audio_track` hard-coded those to all-on. The device
  track now takes its `AudioSourceOptions`, and `LocalAudioTrack` passes
  the switches PlatformAudio was last configured with.

`LkRuntime` remembers the switches, `configure_audio_processing` and the
three `set_*` conveniences apply them (to the APM at once, and to every
device track created from then on), and `active_*_type()` answers `None`
for a stage that is off rather than `Software` unconditionally.
